#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128835 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "000408ba-6fbd-51da-9002-82fdde167a1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Point Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuities Exist"}] :question/id #uuid "6e182a7d-e877-546b-9ee2-1dc2a37e0fbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-3579" :question/problem "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a removable discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/8abe220313dcb5097dcb66f6b13d2190.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3$. Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x) \\neq f(3)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity. A similar argument applies for $x=3$, which is also a removable discontinuity. So, the function has removable discontinuities at $x=-3, 3$.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=0$, but it is not a removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(0)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=0$ is not a removable discontinuity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128836 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff61d37b-595f-5dce-b4d5-fe70b3cf1d54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Point Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "35f49da3-296d-5b2a-8901-1650f6238468" :question/math-academy-id "e-3580" :question/problem "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a point discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/10672c90c91cf5d9de3a618d768abfd2.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2$. Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) \\neq f(2)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a point discontinuity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128837 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties Related to Point Discontinuities Given a Function Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "d5f60234-7254-5707-926a-8f7b7b15e92a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3581" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)$, defined by\n${x + 3,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x = 0; 3 - x, \\mid x > 0$,\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^+} f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^-} f(x)$\n2. $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)=f(0)$\n3. $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n$lim_(x → 0^{-})f(x) = lim_(x → 0^{-})(x + 3) = 3,; lim_(x → 0^{+})f(x) = lim_(x → 0^{+})(3 - x) = 3$.\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n$\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3. \\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- The value of the limit is $\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3$, while the value of the function is $f(0)=0$. So, statement II is false and\n$\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)\\neq f(0)\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$, and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129733 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff61d37b-595f-5dce-b4d5-fe70b3cf1d54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Point Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b67527a-f4e3-5391-a905-44fcbfa0c672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/q-48115-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128193} {:answer/id #uuid "a876e01a-f3b7-5747-aca5-51b1fca57206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/q-48115-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128194} {:answer/id #uuid "db976f25-01f6-5b75-ae8c-da560e14b229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/q-48115-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128195} {:answer/id #uuid "16df2d72-856b-5be2-8cd2-6a2abe45ba08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/q-48115-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128196} {:answer/id #uuid "56da6571-83cb-5c12-b88e-df10c545f97f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/q-48115-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b67527a-f4e3-5391-a905-44fcbfa0c672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/q-48115-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128193} :answer-field/id #uuid "55be8106-456e-5f7d-9b4e-e58db69282d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128192}] :question/id #uuid "89af0137-085e-52c8-8599-1a8b5ce6239a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48115" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has a removable discontinuity at $x = 3$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties Related to Point Discontinuities Given a Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86978994-32c7-540e-9c72-afdae08db11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128433} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d5ee4d-89d9-50cc-80f6-86fa63e36b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128434} {:answer/id #uuid "f80e4162-7bf6-506d-a86d-3a92205369a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128435} {:answer/id #uuid "52080794-e2d5-596e-8b69-ce656836e0c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128436} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4d2e19-f4e5-5079-a4c2-8e82c75ec276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f80e4162-7bf6-506d-a86d-3a92205369a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128435} :answer-field/id #uuid "54fef6ec-5613-5739-be97-f0d1c6191169" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128432}] :question/id #uuid "de45d0f1-1f5a-51b7-a854-73bfcba5bbdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-71181" :question/problem "Given that\n$g(x)|= {5x - 5,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1; x^{3} - 1, \\mid x > 1$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $lim_(x → 1^{+})g(x) = lim_(x → 1^{-})g(x)$\n2. $lim_(x → 1)g(x) = g(1)$\n3. $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x = 1$"}]}
